    $astroSun_master$> Last chapter established the yardstick — the astronomical unit — and the boundary that divides our system in two, the snow line. This chapter turns to the object at the centre of it all: the Sun. But not as a star to be dissected — the fusion physics of the solar interior belongs to stellar physics, a later block. Our question here is local and different: **what does the Sun *do* to this system?** The answer is four things, and each shapes everything else. It **dominates gravitationally**, holding 99.87% of the mass and organizing the planets into clean, near-independent orbits. It **sets the temperature gradient**, through the inverse-square fall of its light — the master variable that put the snow line at 2.7 AU and makes Venus an oven and Neptune a frozen waste. It **blows a wind** of charged particles that sweeps past every planet, stripping unprotected atmospheres and lighting aurorae. And it **defines the system's outer boundary** — or rather two of them, a wind boundary and a gravity boundary, that differ by a factor of nearly a thousand. This is the chapter where the Sun stops being a light in the sky and becomes what it is: the engine, the furnace, the gravitational authority, and the weather system of the entire solar system.

## The morning the Sun set the telegraphs on fire

On the morning of 1 September 1859, an English amateur astronomer named Richard Carrington was doing what he did most clear mornings: projecting an image of the Sun onto a screen in his private observatory south of London, and carefully sketching the sunspots. Patient, unglamorous work — the sort that occasionally, without warning, puts someone at the centre of history.

At about 11:18, within the group of sunspots he was drawing, **two brilliant beads of blinding white light appeared** — far brighter than the blazing solar surface around them. Carrington's first thought was that a hole had opened in his apparatus and admitted raw sunlight. He checked; nothing was wrong. He ran to fetch a witness; by the time he returned, the beads had faded. The whole event lasted about five minutes. Carrington had just become the first human being to observe a **solar flare**.

Then the Sun's message arrived. About **seventeen and a half hours later**, the Earth was struck by the most intense geomagnetic storm in recorded history. **Aurorae — normally confined to the poles — blazed as far south as Cuba, Hawaii, and Rome.** In the northeastern United States the glow was reportedly bright enough to read a newspaper by at midnight; gold miners in the Rockies rose and began cooking breakfast, assuming dawn had come. And the young global telegraph network — the "Victorian internet," the first technology to string long conductors across the landscape — failed spectacularly, and then failed in ways that were frankly eerie. Operators reported sparks leaping from their equipment; some took electric shocks; there are accounts of telegraph paper catching fire. Most uncanny of all, some operators **disconnected their batteries entirely and kept sending messages**, the lines carrying enough current induced by the storm to work on their own.

{{image: Solar flare | A solar flare erupting from the Sun's surface — a sudden release of magnetic energy, the same kind of event Richard Carrington first witnessed as two beads of white light in 1859. A flare's radiation crosses to Earth in eight minutes; the coronal mass ejection that often follows takes one to three days, and when it arrives it can drive currents through every long conductor on the planet. The Sun is not a passive lamp — it has weather, and its weather reaches us.}}

Why open a lecture on the Sun this way? Because it makes visceral a fact otherwise easy to overlook: **the Sun is not a distant, passive lamp. It reaches out and touches us.** It has weather; it has outbursts; it exhales a wind that permanently envelops every planet, and when that wind gusts, things happen here, eight light-minutes downstream. In 1859 the cost was sparks in telegraph offices. For a civilization now built on power grids, satellites, and GPS, a Carrington-class event would run to the trillions — and this is not hypothetical: in July 2012 a comparable ejection crossed Earth's orbital path and missed us by about nine days of orbital motion. We simply were not there when it went by. Let us start with the most basic form of the Sun's authority: its mass.

## Gravitational authority: why one body rules

Begin with the simplest and most consequential fact about the Sun: **it contains essentially all the mass in the solar system.** Its mass is $1.989 \times 10^{30}$ kilograms; the eight planets combined come to $2.667 \times 10^{27}$. The ratio is stark.

> **The Sun outweighs all eight planets combined by a factor of 746.**
> **It constitutes 99.87% of the mass of the solar system.**

Everything else — every planet, moon, asteroid, comet, and grain of dust, everything we will spend the rest of this block studying — makes up **0.13%**, and within that sliver Jupiter alone holds 71%. The Sun is physically enormous to match: 109 times Earth's diameter, with room inside for 1.3 million Earths.

{{image: Sun | The Sun in extreme detail — a churning ball of plasma 109 times Earth's width, holding 99.87% of the solar system's mass. Every planet, moon, asteroid, and comet we will study is a trace impurity orbiting in its gravitational well. That single fact of mass dominance is why the system is a set of clean, near-independent orbits rather than a chaotic tangle: the Sun does not merely light the solar system, it organizes it.}}

This dominance is not just a striking statistic; it is *why our solar system has the structure it does.* Newton showed that two bodies orbit their common centre of mass, so strictly the Sun does not sit fixed while a planet circles it — both orbit a shared point. But when one body outweighs everything else 746-fold, that shared centre lies **essentially inside the Sun**, and the Sun's motion is a small wobble rather than a comparable orbit. This is why Kepler's laws work so beautifully here: to an excellent approximation, each planet really does orbit a fixed central mass.

More deeply, the dominance means **each planet's orbit is governed almost entirely by the Sun and only slightly by the other planets.** Jupiter tugs on Saturn, and these perturbations are real — they are how Neptune was discovered, and they will turn out to have rearranged the whole system. But they are small corrections to a motion set by one central body. The solar system is therefore, to first order, a set of **nearly independent two-body problems** — eight planets each in its own private conversation with the Sun — rather than a chaotic eight-body tangle. This is not guaranteed: among the exoplanet systems we now observe, planets are often comparable in mass, or a second star provides a rival authority, and such systems are far less orderly and often unstable. Our system's clean, near-circular, near-coplanar architecture is a direct consequence of one body holding 99.87% of the mass. **The Sun does not merely light the system; it organizes it.**

## The flux gradient: why distance sets temperature

We come now to the property that matters most for this block — the one that put the snow line at 2.7 AU and thereby divided the solar system in two. And it is, pleasingly, a direct application of the inverse-square law we met in its gravitational form.

The Sun radiates at a fixed rate — its **luminosity**, $L_\odot = 3.83 \times 10^{26}$ watts, roughly a trillion times the total power output of human civilization. That energy streams outward in all directions. Consider a sphere of radius $r$ centred on the Sun: *all* of the Sun's output crosses that sphere, whatever its size — but the sphere's area grows as $4\pi r^2$. So the energy through each square metre, the **flux** $F$, falls off as the inverse square of distance:

$$F = \frac{L_\odot}{4\pi r^2}$$

This is exactly the geometry behind gravity's inverse-square law: an influence spreading uniformly in all directions dilutes over the surface of an expanding sphere. Light does the same. At Earth's distance the flux has a name — the **solar constant** — and its value is **1,361 watts per square metre**, about a kilowatt and a third, which is why solar panels perform as they do. Now run that formula across the whole system and watch the consequences of the last chapter's distances become concrete:

```fluxgradient
```

Look at the range. **Mercury receives 6.7 times the sunlight Earth does; Neptune receives about one part in nine hundred of it** — a factor of roughly **6,000** from the innermost planet to the outermost, imposed by nothing but geometry. This is the single steepest environmental gradient in the solar system, and three consequences follow that together make it the master variable of the block.

**First, it sets every world's temperature** — modified by albedo (how much sunlight a body reflects) and greenhouse effects (how much heat its atmosphere traps). Earth's actual mean surface temperature is 288 K rather than the 278 K of pure equilibrium, thanks to a modest greenhouse; Venus's is a hellish 737 K rather than 327 K, the single most dramatic result in comparative planetology. But the flux is the baseline on which all those modifications operate. **Second, it created the snow line.** Because the young disk's temperature followed this same falling gradient, there was a distance — around 2.7 AU — inside which water could not freeze and outside which it could, sorting the system into rocky worlds and giants. The most important structural fact about our planetary system is a consequence of the inverse-square law applied to sunlight. **Third, it drives everything else that varies with distance** — which ices a body can keep, why comets grow tails only when close, why the outer moons are icy and the inner ones rock, and why probes beyond Jupiter carry nuclear batteries rather than solar panels.

```example
solar-flux
```

**Distance sets flux; flux sets temperature; temperature sets chemistry; chemistry sets what worlds can form and keep.** That is the causal chain of this block, and this is where its first two arrows come from.

## A tour of the Sun: from core to corona

Before we follow the wind outward, we need a working picture of the object producing it. The Sun has no solid surface anywhere; it is plasma — gas so hot that electrons are stripped from nuclei — throughout, changing continuously from centre to edge. The **core** (the inner quarter) is where the energy is made: at some 15 million kelvin, hydrogen fuses into helium, converting about **4.3 million tonnes of mass into energy every second**. The **radiative zone** carries that energy outward as photons, so endlessly absorbed and re-emitted that the journey from core to surface takes on the order of a hundred thousand years — the light warming your face left the core before our species existed. The **convective zone**, the outer third, carries energy by great rising and sinking cells of plasma, like boiling water; this churning, combined with the Sun's rotation, generates its magnetic field. The **photosphere** is the visible "surface" from which light finally escapes freely, at about **5,772 K** — only a few hundred kilometres thick, which is why the gaseous Sun appears to have a sharp edge. Sunspots live here.

{{image: Solar corona | The Sun's corona, revealed during a total solar eclipse as a pearly halo streaming far beyond the hidden disk. Though it lies above the 5,772 K visible surface, the corona reaches one to three million kelvin — hundreds of times hotter than the surface beneath it. Why remains one of the great open questions in solar physics: heat should flow from hot to cold, yet moving outward, away from the energy source, the temperature soars. It is also so hot that the Sun's gravity cannot hold it — which is the origin of the solar wind.}}

Above the photosphere lie the tenuous **chromosphere** and then the **corona** — and here we meet something genuinely strange. The photosphere is 5,772 K; the corona above it reaches **one to three million kelvin, two to five hundred times hotter than the surface beneath it.** Stop and notice how bizarre this is. Heat flows from hot to cold; move away from a fire and you cool. But move outward from the Sun's surface, *away* from the energy source, and the temperature soars by a factor of hundreds. This is the **coronal heating problem**, and it is worth flagging clearly: **it is not fully solved.** Recognized since the 1940s, it remains an active frontier; the leading candidates involve the magnetic field — nanoflares (countless tiny reconnection events) and Alfvén waves (magnetic waves from the churning below) — and NASA's Parker Solar Probe is flying repeatedly through the corona itself to find out. I raise it partly because it is interesting and partly for calibration: **the Sun is the best-observed star in the universe, eight light-minutes away, watched continuously by a fleet of spacecraft — and we still cannot fully say why its atmosphere is hundreds of times hotter than its surface.** That should temper how much we claim to know about far more distant objects. The corona matters here for one concrete reason: **it is so hot that the Sun's gravity cannot hold it.** Which brings us to the wind.

## The solar wind: the Sun exhales

The corona's million-degree plasma moves so fast that a portion escapes the Sun entirely and streams outward through the whole system. This continuous outflow is the **solar wind** — a flow of charged particles, mostly protons and electrons, blowing radially outward at roughly **400 km/s** (the slow wind) to **750 km/s** (the fast wind from coronal holes), and carrying the Sun's magnetic field along with it. Its density at Earth is only about **5 particles per cubic centimetre** — *a better vacuum than anything we can make in a laboratory.* It is not a gale in any ordinary sense; it is an exceedingly thin stream of very fast particles. The Sun sheds roughly **1.5 billion kilograms per second** to it — enormous by human standards, trivial against the Sun.

```example
solar-wind-travel
```

Despite its thinness the wind matters enormously, because it never stops, it is electrically charged, and over billions of years its cumulative effect is profound. It **carves out the heliosphere**, the vast bubble in which the Sun's plasma, not the galaxy's, sets conditions. It **shapes cometary tails** — a comet's ion tail points directly away from the Sun regardless of the comet's motion, because the wind blows it; this was the very clue that led to the wind's prediction before it was ever detected. And, most consequentially for the worlds ahead, **it strips unprotected atmospheres.** When the wind meets a planet with no magnetic shield, it interacts directly with the upper atmosphere and can accelerate particles to escape velocity, eroding the air into space over eons. This is, on current evidence, a principal reason **Mars is a cold, dry desert today** — a story we develop fully when we reach it.

## Space weather and magnetospheres: the Sun's reach

The wind is not steady. The Sun's magnetic field, generated by the churning convective zone, rises and falls on an **11-year cycle** — a near-featureless disk at minimum, peppered with sunspots at maximum, where intense fields suppress convection and leave patches cooler and darker than their surroundings. Sunspots store magnetic energy, and that energy can release suddenly, in two related but distinct events. A **solar flare** is a burst of electromagnetic radiation from reconnecting field lines — what Carrington saw — travelling at light speed, so it arrives eight minutes after the event with no warning. A **coronal mass ejection (CME)** is a physical expulsion of *billions of tonnes* of plasma, threaded with magnetic field, hurled out at hundreds to thousands of km/s; a CME aimed at Earth usually arrives one to three days later — the Carrington CME made the crossing in about 17.6 hours, which is why it was so severe. When a CME strikes, it can drive currents through long conductors — power lines, pipelines, telegraph wires. A 1989 storm blacked out Québec's grid for nine hours; the 2012 near-miss showed such events are not once-in-a-civilization rarities.

Here is the part that sets up the worlds to come. **A planet with a global magnetic field carves out a protected cavity in the solar wind — a *magnetosphere* — that deflects the charged particles around it rather than letting them reach the atmosphere.** Earth has one, generated by convection in its liquid-iron core; the wind is diverted around us, and the particles that do leak in are funnelled to the poles, where they strike the upper air and make it glow — **the aurorae**, the visible signature of the shield at work. During the Carrington Event the shield was so overwhelmed that the glow reached the tropics.

But the comparative question is the whole point: **the planets differ enormously in whether they have this shield, and the differences correlate with their fates** — though not simply. Earth has a strong field, a thick atmosphere, and liquid water. Venus has *no* global field yet keeps a crushing atmosphere (its sheer mass compensates). Mars has no field today, though magnetized ancient rocks show it once did — and it has lost most of its air. Mercury has a real field but negligible atmosphere for other reasons. Jupiter has the most powerful magnetosphere of all, generated by metallic hydrogen, with the brightest aurorae in the system. Notice the correlation is suggestive but not deterministic: Venus keeps an atmosphere without a field, Mercury lacks one despite having a field. **Magnetic shielding is one factor among several** — planetary mass, distance from the Sun, atmospheric composition, and the timing of a field's loss all matter — and sorting out how they combine is one of the central tasks of comparative planetology. But it is a real and important factor, and the Sun's wind is the reason it matters at all.

## Where does the solar system end? Two very different answers

We finish by following the wind all the way out, to a question that sounds elementary and turns out to be genuinely subtle: **where does the solar system end?** It has two defensible answers, separated by a factor of nearly a thousand, and the gap between them is the lesson.

```solarboundaries
```

Track the Sun's **wind**, and the edge is the **heliopause**, at about **120 AU**, where the wind's outward push finally balances the pressure of the interstellar medium. The boundary has structure — a termination shock near 75–90 AU where the wind abruptly slows, a turbulent heliosheath beyond, and the heliopause itself where the solar plasma gives way to the galaxy's. And here is one of the great achievements of exploration: **we have measured it directly. Voyager 1 crossed the heliopause in August 2012 at 121 AU; Voyager 2 followed in November 2018 at 119 AU** — two craft launched in 1977 with 1970s electronics, flying for decades, that sent back the exact moment they passed out of the Sun's wind and into interstellar space. They remain the only human objects to have done it, and they are still transmitting.

But track the Sun's **gravity** instead — ask where objects cease to orbit the Sun — and the boundary lies enormously farther out. The **Oort Cloud**, the vast spherical shell of icy bodies that supplies our long-period comets, extends to something like **100,000 AU**. Those bodies are gravitationally bound to the Sun; they orbit it on million-year paths; by any reasonable definition they are part of the solar system. And 100,000 AU is **1.58 light-years** — a substantial fraction of the way to the nearest star.

```example
escape-boundaries
```

Why do the two boundaries differ so wildly? Because they track different influences with different ranges. The heliopause is a *pressure balance* between two thin plasmas, which fails where the wind has thinned by spreading over an enormous sphere. The Oort Cloud's edge is *gravitational competition* with the galaxy — and gravity, falling only as $1/r^2$ and never shielded or cancelled, reaches vastly farther. **So the Voyagers have simultaneously "left the solar system" and are nowhere near leaving it.** They are outside the wind's bubble, in interstellar plasma — and still deep inside the Sun's gravitational domain, with the Oort Cloud's outer reaches a thousand times farther ahead. At their speed, Voyager 1 will take roughly 300 years just to reach the inner Oort Cloud and tens of thousands to pass through it. I like this question because it captures something true about scientific categories: **"where does X end?" often has no single answer, because the boundary depends on which physical influence you choose to track.** That is not a failure of the science; it reflects the fact that a star's influence takes several forms with very different ranges — the same lesson that returns when we argue over what counts as a planet.

## The engine at the centre

The Sun will now appear in essentially every remaining chapter of this block, so let its role be clear. It holds **99.87%** of the system's mass, which is why the centre of mass sits inside it, why Kepler's laws work as though planets orbit a fixed point, and why our system is a set of orderly, nearly independent orbits rather than a tangle — the Sun *organizes* the system. It imposes the **flux gradient**, $F = L_\odot/4\pi r^2$, running from 668% of Earth's sunlight at Mercury to 0.11% at Neptune — a factor of 6,000 across the system, setting every world's baseline temperature and creating the snow line that split it into rocky worlds and giants. Its million-kelvin corona, too hot to hold, escapes as the **solar wind** — thin, relentless, charged — which carves the heliosphere, blows cometary tails, drives space weather, and strips the atmospheres of unshielded worlds. And its domain has two edges: the **heliopause** at ~120 AU, crossed by the Voyagers, and the **gravitational** edge at the Oort Cloud, 833 times farther still.

Here is what to carry forward. In the chapters ahead we will study worlds — their surfaces, atmospheres, interiors, and moons — and it will be tempting to treat each as self-contained. Resist that. **Every one of them sits in an environment defined by the Sun**: held by its gravity, warmed by a flux that falls as the inverse square of distance, and buffeted by a wind that has been blowing past it for four and a half billion years. When Venus turns out to be an oven and Mars a desert, when the giants turn out to be gas-rich and the inner worlds bare rock, when comets grow tails and Jupiter's aurorae outshine everything — the Sun is in the explanation. It is not the background of this block. It is 99.87% of it.$astroSun_master$,
